#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Characterize engine wall-clock time across the parameter space.

Measures how `engine_backend="lineal"`/`"generational"` (both
`jit="off"` and `jit="numba"`)/`"generational-vector"` scale as one
`SimulationParams` field sweeps across a range of values, holding every
other field fixed. `L_1` -- a single-lineage (`n_replicates=1`)
`"lineal"` run at the *same* swept value -- is a pure normalization
divisor, not itself a series to plot: every other column is reported
both in raw seconds and as a ratio to `L_1`, so the reported numbers
answer "how many single-lineage-run-equivalents does this batch cost,"
machine-independent in the sense that the ratio, not the absolute time,
is the number worth comparing across machines/sessions. `L_batch`
(`"lineal"` run at the *same* batch size as every other engine measured
here) is the real fourth data series -- how the reference engine itself
scales, on the same footing as G and V, not left implicit inside what
they get divided by. Four backend/JIT combinations are measured at
every point: `L_batch`, `"generational"` with `jit="off"`,
`"generational"` with `jit="numba"`, and `"generational-vector"`
(always effectively JIT'd; has no `jit="off"` mode) -- enough to plot
t(sweep value) for every engine and JIT setting this project currently
offers.

Default sweep is `d` (deme count) -- the axis `auto_vector_min_d`
(`fim.engine`) already exists to threshold, and the one the project
owner asked this tool to characterize first. `--sweep N|mu|m|
loci-length|replicates` characterizes the other candidate scaling
factors named directly (population size, mutation/migration rate, locus
length/capacity, batch size) -- run each independently; this tool does
not attempt a full multi-factor grid (out of scope, and combinatorially
large). `--sweep replicates` varies `L_batch`/G/V's own batch size
(`n_replicates`) directly via `--values`, holding `d`/`N`/`mu`/`m`/
locus length at the fixed baseline and `L_1` at its own always-`n_
replicates=1` reference point regardless -- the one field this tool
could already vary via `--replicates` for every *other* sweep, now
itself sweepable, per this project's own multi-model engine review,
2026-09-04, Tier E's unnamed "benchmarking-process" finding (GPT-5.6;
`20260904-claude-sonnet-5-fim-engine-review-remediations.md` Phase 7
item 7): "does not sweep the product of replicas, loci, demes, and
capacity." This extension covers the two capabilities that finding and
Phase 7 item 7 name explicitly -- a replica-count sweep and peak-RSS
capture; the finding's own fuller six-scenario-class matrix (zero/
migration-only/mutation-only/both; low/med/high active-deme mutation;
1/several/many loci; bounded/unbounded execution; symmetric/explicit
matrices; capacity sweeps near the configured budget) is a
substantially larger scope than a single Tier E line item, and is not
attempted here -- each of those six is already reachable by composing
this tool's own existing `--sweep`/`--config`/`--values` options by
hand, just not yet packaged as a named preset.

`--measure-rss` additionally captures each backend's own peak resident
set size, in a fresh subprocess per backend per point (`--_rss-worker`,
this same script re-invoked with `sys.executable`) -- RSS is a
monotonic high-water mark for the life of a process, so measuring two
backends, or two points, back to back in one process would report
whichever ran first's own peak polluting whichever ran after it, not
either one's real peak (the same reasoning this project's own `FIM-54`
remediation gives for why any peak-RSS before/after comparison needs
one process per measurement, unlike the timing path's own safe
median-of-N-trials-in-one-process harness). Off by default -- every
existing invocation with no `--measure-rss` pays no extra subprocess
cost and sees no RSS columns, identical to before this option existed.

`"generational"` here means exactly what `fim(engine_backend=
"generational")` gives a real caller: `GenerationalBackend(
ThreadedAdvancer())`, default `max_workers` (`os.cpu_count()`) --
deliberately not an idealized single-threaded measurement, so this
tool's own G numbers already reflect whatever real thread-count
behavior `ThreadedAdvancer` actually has on this machine, regressions
included (see `20260901-claude-sonnet-5-fim-engine-backend-factory-
design.md` S11's own "System-resource behavior" finding: post-Stage-F8,
`ThreadedAdvancer` delivers little to no real speedup at this project's
own reference scale -- this tool's own G/L_1 ratio is expected to show
that directly, not hide it behind an idealized number).

Every point is the median of `--trials` independent, freshly-built runs
(not re-timing a warm cache) -- median-of-N, matching this project's
own established sweep methodology (the d>=35 crossover finding used the
same discipline after a first, discarded single-trial attempt showed
30-40% run-to-run swings). `"generational"` with `jit="numba"` and
`"generational-vector"` each pay their own one-time numba JIT compile
cost on first use -- two independent compiled functions, not one; this
tool issues one untimed warm-up call before each one's own first
measurement so neither cost ever contaminates a reported trial (the
exact mistake this project's own history already made once and had to
catch).

"Machine-saturated" (the project owner's own phrasing): this tool does
not itself pin CPU affinity or launch background load -- it assumes the
caller runs it on an otherwise-idle machine (this project's own
established sweep discipline: "run in isolation, no unrelated process
competing for CPU") and records `os.cpu_count()`/`os.getloadavg()` in
its own output as a fingerprint of what "saturated" meant for that run,
so a future comparison can tell whether two runs are actually
comparable rather than assuming so.

`--output` writes a full evidence file for whoever runs this, but its
own results (unlike `calibrate-statistical-bands`'s own JSON) are not
committed to this repository -- machine-specific timing numbers would
go stale the moment anyone runs this on different hardware, exactly
the reasoning `auto_vector_min_d`'s own docstring already gives for
being a caller-supplied parameter rather than a hardcoded constant.
Findings worth keeping get written up as prose (this project's own
established practice for every prior benchmark sweep), not as a
versioned raw-numbers artifact.

`--config` overrides the fixed (non-swept) baseline -- `N`, `d`, `m`,
`mu`, and locus length -- from a real `fim` YAML config file, the exact
same format and `SimulationParams.from_mapping` validation `fim run`
itself uses, rather than this tool's own compiled-in reference-scale
constants (`DEFAULT_D`/`DEFAULT_N`/`DEFAULT_MU`/`DEFAULT_M`/`DEFAULT_
LOCUS_LENGTH`, still used for whichever of those a config does not
mention, and for every field when no `--config` is given at all --
every existing invocation with no `--config` behaves identically to
before this option existed). The config's own value for whichever
field `--sweep` is actually varying is simply never consulted -- `
--values` always drives that one. The config must describe exactly one
locus (this tool has only ever modeled one) and a scalar, symmetric
`m`/`N`/`mu` (a caller-supplied migration matrix, or a per-deme `N`, or
a per-locus `mu`, all valid in a real `fim` config, have no single
scalar this tool could hold fixed while sweeping something else) --
rejected with a clear error, not silently narrowed to one entry, if the
config supplies either.

Usage:
    dev/bin/benchmark-engines --sweep d \\
        --values 4,10,20,35,50,80,120 --replicates 16 \\
        --generations 100 --trials 3 \\
        --config my-baseline.yaml \\
        --output /tmp/benchmark-d-sweep.json
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import platform
import resource
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from collections.abc import Mapping
from dataclasses import dataclass, replace
from datetime import UTC, datetime
from importlib.metadata import version as installed_version
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any, NamedTuple

import yaml

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
sys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT / "src"))

from fim.engine import (  # noqa: E402
    EngineBackendChoice,
    JitOption,
    build_engine_backend,
)
from fim.model.locus import LocusSpec  # noqa: E402
from fim.model.params import SimulationParams  # noqa: E402
from fim.persistence.store import InMemoryTrajectoryStore  # noqa: E402

SweepField = str  # one of "d", "N", "mu", "m", "loci-length", "replicates"

DEFAULT_D = 60
DEFAULT_N = 500
DEFAULT_MU = 0.001
DEFAULT_M = 0.05
DEFAULT_LOCUS_LENGTH = 4  # capacity = 4**4 = 256, this project's own reference scale


class FixedBaseline(NamedTuple):
    """The five fields held fixed for whichever one `--sweep` is not varying.

    Built once per invocation (`_load_fixed_baseline`), from either
    `--config` or this module's own `DEFAULT_*` constants -- never
    re-read per swept value, since none of these five ever changes
    within one sweep (only the swept field's own value does, per point,
    in `_measure_point`).
    """

    d: int
    n: int
    mu: float
    m: float
    locus_length: int


def _load_fixed_baseline(config_path: Path | None) -> FixedBaseline:
    """Return the fixed baseline from `--config`, or this module's own defaults.

    `config_path=None` (no `--config` given) reproduces this tool's own
    original, hardcoded-constant behavior exactly -- every sweep run
    before this option existed is unaffected.

    Args:
        config_path: A real `fim` YAML config file, or `None`.

    Returns:
        The five fixed-baseline values, ready to feed `_measure_point`.

    Raises:
        ValueError: If the config's own `N`, `m`, or `mu` is not a
            plain scalar (a per-deme `N`, a migration matrix, or a
            per-locus `mu` -- all valid in a real `fim` config, none of
            them a single fixed value this tool could hold while
            sweeping something else), or if it describes anything other
            than exactly one locus.
    """
    if config_path is None:
        return FixedBaseline(
            d=DEFAULT_D,
            n=DEFAULT_N,
            mu=DEFAULT_MU,
            m=DEFAULT_M,
            locus_length=DEFAULT_LOCUS_LENGTH,
        )
    payload = yaml.safe_load(config_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    if not isinstance(payload, Mapping):
        raise ValueError(f"{config_path}: configuration root must be a mapping")
    params = SimulationParams.from_mapping(payload)
    if not isinstance(params.N, int):
        raise ValueError(f"{config_path}: N must be a single scalar, not per-deme")
    if not isinstance(params.m, int | float):
        raise ValueError(f"{config_path}: m must be a scalar rate, not a matrix")
    if not isinstance(params.mu, int | float):
        raise ValueError(f"{config_path}: mu must be a single scalar, not per-locus")
    if len(params.loci) != 1:
        raise ValueError(
            f"{config_path}: must describe exactly one locus, got {len(params.loci)}"
        )
    return FixedBaseline(
        d=params.d,
        n=params.N,
        mu=params.mu,
        m=params.m,
        locus_length=params.loci[0].length,
    )


def _clock() -> datetime:
    """Return a fixed manifest timestamp -- irrelevant to a timing run."""
    return datetime(2026, 9, 2, tzinfo=UTC)


@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class BenchmarkPoint:
    """One swept value's own measured backend times, in seconds.

    `lineal_single_seconds` (`L_1`) is a pure normalization divisor, not
    itself a series to plot -- `lineal_batch_seconds` (`L_batch`, the
    same batch size every other engine is measured at, run through
    `"lineal"` itself) is the real fourth data series: how the
    reference engine's own batch cost scales, on the same axis as G and
    V, not hidden behind L_1's own role as a denominator.
    """

    swept_field: str
    swept_value: float
    d: int
    lineal_single_seconds: float
    lineal_batch_seconds: float
    generational_jit_off_seconds: float | None
    generational_jit_numba_seconds: float | None
    vector_seconds: float | None
    vector_skip_reason: str | None
    # Populated only when `--measure-rss` is given; `None` on every field
    # otherwise -- `_print_table`/`_render_data` gate their own RSS
    # columns on that same flag, not on inspecting whether any of these
    # happen to be `None` (a real Backend-V skip and "RSS was never
    # measured at all" must stay visibly different).
    lineal_batch_rss_bytes: int | None = None
    generational_jit_off_rss_bytes: int | None = None
    generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes: int | None = None
    vector_rss_bytes: int | None = None


def _base_params(
    *,
    d: int = DEFAULT_D,
    N: int = DEFAULT_N,
    mu: float = DEFAULT_MU,
    m: float = DEFAULT_M,
    locus_length: int = DEFAULT_LOCUS_LENGTH,
    n_replicates: int,
    max_generations: int,
    seed: int,
) -> SimulationParams:
    """Build a reference-scale `SimulationParams`, one field at a time overridable.

    `replicate_tolerance=None` explicitly, not `SimulationParams`'s own
    current default (a real number) -- a benchmark needs every point to
    run an exact, fixed replicate count, never an adaptive early stop
    that would make the very thing being timed vary from run to run.
    """
    return SimulationParams(
        N=N,
        m=m,
        mu=mu,
        d=d,
        seed=seed,
        loci=(LocusSpec(1, locus_length),),
        mutation_model="finite_alleles",
        migrant_sampling="continuous",
        max_generations=max_generations,
        n_replicates=n_replicates,
        replicate_tolerance=None,
        convergence_tolerance=0.0,
        convergence_window=max_generations + 1,
    )


def _run_once(
    engine_backend: EngineBackendChoice,
    params: SimulationParams,
    *,
    jit: JitOption = "off",
) -> float:
    """Run one full batch, return elapsed wall-clock seconds."""
    backend = build_engine_backend(engine_backend, jit=jit)
    store = InMemoryTrajectoryStore()
    start = time.perf_counter()
    backend.run(params, store, None, _clock)
    return time.perf_counter() - start


def _median_of(
    engine_backend: EngineBackendChoice,
    params: SimulationParams,
    trials: int,
    *,
    jit: JitOption = "off",
) -> float:
    """Median wall-clock seconds across `trials` independent runs."""
    samples = [_run_once(engine_backend, params, jit=jit) for _ in range(trials)]
    return statistics.median(samples)


def _ru_maxrss_bytes(ru_maxrss: int) -> int:
    """Normalize `resource.getrusage(...).ru_maxrss` to bytes across platforms.

    Linux reports `ru_maxrss` in kibibytes; macOS (and other BSD-derived
    platforms) report it in bytes directly -- the same well-known POSIX
    `getrusage` platform discrepancy this project's own `FIM-54`
    remediation measured around ad hoc, on a single machine, where the
    unit could stay implicit because it cancelled out in a same-machine
    before/after ratio. A reusable tool meant to run on both this
    development machine (macOS) and citrus-2 (Linux) cannot leave it
    implicit -- a raw, unnormalized comparison across those two would
    silently read as a ~1000x difference that is really just the unit.
    """
    return ru_maxrss if platform.system() == "Darwin" else ru_maxrss * 1024


def _run_rss_worker(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    """Run one backend/params combination once; print its own peak RSS in bytes.

    The `--_rss-worker` entry point `_measure_rss` re-invokes this same
    script as a fresh subprocess for -- internal-use only, not part of
    this tool's own public CLI surface (no `--help` text of its own;
    `main` dispatches here before building the real argument parser).
    One run, not `_median_of`'s own several trials: a subprocess-per-
    measurement design already pays real fork/interpreter-startup
    overhead per point, and unlike wall-clock time, RSS has no
    meaningful "median across trials" -- the JIT-compiled backends'
    first-ever compile in a *fresh* process is itself part of what a
    real invocation of this configuration would pay, not warm-up noise
    to discard.
    """
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    parser.add_argument("--engine-backend", required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--jit", default="off")
    parser.add_argument("--d", type=int, required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--n", type=int, required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--mu", type=float, required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--m", type=float, required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--locus-length", type=int, required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--n-replicates", type=int, required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--generations", type=int, required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--seed", type=int, required=True)
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    params = _base_params(
        d=args.d,
        N=args.n,
        mu=args.mu,
        m=args.m,
        locus_length=args.locus_length,
        n_replicates=args.n_replicates,
        max_generations=args.generations,
        seed=args.seed,
    )
    _run_once(args.engine_backend, params, jit=args.jit)
    peak_bytes = _ru_maxrss_bytes(resource.getrusage(resource.RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss)
    print(peak_bytes)
    return 0


def _measure_rss(
    engine_backend: EngineBackendChoice,
    params: SimulationParams,
    *,
    jit: JitOption = "off",
) -> int | None:
    """Peak RSS in bytes for one backend/params combination, via a fresh subprocess.

    Returns `None` on any worker failure -- Backend V outside its own
    enforced `(d, capacity)` scope, or `numba` unavailable, the same two
    cases `_measure_point`'s own timing path already turns into `None`/
    `vector_skip_reason` -- so RSS measurement never raises where timing
    measurement would silently skip instead; the caller's own existing
    skip decision, not a new independent one, is what governs whether a
    point has real numbers here.
    """
    locus_length = params.loci[0].length
    command = [
        sys.executable,
        str(Path(__file__).resolve()),
        "--_rss-worker",
        "--engine-backend",
        engine_backend,
        "--jit",
        jit,
        "--d",
        str(params.d),
        "--n",
        str(params.N),
        "--mu",
        str(params.mu),
        "--m",
        str(params.m),
        "--locus-length",
        str(locus_length),
        "--n-replicates",
        str(params.n_replicates),
        "--generations",
        str(params.max_generations),
        "--seed",
        str(params.seed),
    ]
    result = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return int(result.stdout.strip())


def _measure_point(
    field: SweepField,
    value: float,
    *,
    baseline: FixedBaseline,
    replicates: int,
    generations: int,
    trials: int,
    seed: int,
    warmed_up_generational_numba: list[bool],
    warmed_up_vector: list[bool],
    measure_rss: bool = False,
) -> BenchmarkPoint:
    """Measure one swept value's own L_1/L_batch/G(off)/G(numba)/V times.

    `warmed_up_generational_numba` and `warmed_up_vector` are two
    separate flags, not one shared flag: `"generational"`'s own
    `jit="numba"` JIT-compiles `drift`'s standalone multinomial draw
    (`fim.model.operators._jit_drift_counts_batched` and friends);
    `"generational-vector"`'s own JIT compiles a different function
    entirely (`fim.model.vectorized`'s own batched decomposition) — two
    independent one-time compile costs, each needing its own untimed
    warm-up call, not one flag standing in for both.

    Args:
        baseline: The four other fields' own fixed values (`--config`,
            or this module's own `DEFAULT_*` constants) -- consulted
            for whichever of `d`/`N`/`mu`/`m`/`locus_length`/`replicates`
            is not `field` itself.
        measure_rss: When `True`, additionally spawn one fresh subprocess
            per backend (`_measure_rss`) to capture peak RSS -- doubles
            this point's own wall-clock cost (one process per backend on
            top of the timing runs already paid), so left opt-in.
    """
    d = int(value) if field == "d" else baseline.d
    n = int(value) if field == "N" else baseline.n
    mu = float(value) if field == "mu" else baseline.mu
    m = float(value) if field == "m" else baseline.m
    locus_length = int(value) if field == "loci-length" else baseline.locus_length
    point_replicates = int(value) if field == "replicates" else replicates
    if field not in {"d", "N", "mu", "m", "loci-length", "replicates"}:
        raise ValueError(f"unknown sweep field: {field}")

    single_params = _base_params(
        d=d,
        N=n,
        mu=mu,
        m=m,
        locus_length=locus_length,
        n_replicates=1,
        max_generations=generations,
        seed=seed,
    )
    batch_params = replace(single_params, n_replicates=point_replicates)

    lineal_single_seconds = _median_of("lineal", single_params, trials)
    lineal_batch_seconds = _median_of("lineal", batch_params, trials)
    generational_jit_off_seconds = _median_of("generational", batch_params, trials)

    generational_jit_numba_seconds: float | None = None
    try:
        if not warmed_up_generational_numba[0]:
            _run_once("generational", batch_params, jit="numba")  # untimed warm-up
            warmed_up_generational_numba[0] = True
        generational_jit_numba_seconds = _median_of(
            "generational", batch_params, trials, jit="numba"
        )
    except ImportError:  # numba not installed
        generational_jit_numba_seconds = None

    vector_seconds: float | None = None
    vector_skip_reason: str | None = None
    try:
        if not warmed_up_vector[0]:
            _run_once("generational-vector", batch_params)  # untimed JIT warm-up
            warmed_up_vector[0] = True
        vector_seconds = _median_of("generational-vector", batch_params, trials)
    except ValueError as exc:  # config outside Backend V's own enforced scope
        vector_skip_reason = str(exc)
    except ImportError as exc:  # numba not installed
        vector_skip_reason = f"numba unavailable: {exc}"

    lineal_batch_rss_bytes: int | None = None
    generational_jit_off_rss_bytes: int | None = None
    generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes: int | None = None
    vector_rss_bytes: int | None = None
    if measure_rss:
        lineal_batch_rss_bytes = _measure_rss("lineal", batch_params)
        generational_jit_off_rss_bytes = _measure_rss("generational", batch_params)
        if generational_jit_numba_seconds is not None:
            generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes = _measure_rss(
                "generational", batch_params, jit="numba"
            )
        if vector_seconds is not None:
            vector_rss_bytes = _measure_rss("generational-vector", batch_params)

    return BenchmarkPoint(
        swept_field=field,
        swept_value=value,
        d=d,
        lineal_single_seconds=lineal_single_seconds,
        lineal_batch_seconds=lineal_batch_seconds,
        generational_jit_off_seconds=generational_jit_off_seconds,
        generational_jit_numba_seconds=generational_jit_numba_seconds,
        vector_seconds=vector_seconds,
        vector_skip_reason=vector_skip_reason,
        lineal_batch_rss_bytes=lineal_batch_rss_bytes,
        generational_jit_off_rss_bytes=generational_jit_off_rss_bytes,
        generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes=generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes,
        vector_rss_bytes=vector_rss_bytes,
    )


def _environment_fingerprint() -> dict[str, object]:
    """Return interpreter/platform/package/load-average fingerprint for this run."""
    fingerprint: dict[str, object] = {
        "python_version": platform.python_version(),
        "platform": platform.platform(),
        "machine": platform.machine(),
        "cpu_count": os.cpu_count(),
    }
    try:
        fingerprint["load_average_1min"] = os.getloadavg()[0]
    except (OSError, AttributeError):  # not available on this platform
        fingerprint["load_average_1min"] = None
    for package in ("numpy", "numba"):
        try:
            fingerprint[f"{package}_version"] = installed_version(package)
        except Exception:  # best-effort: a fingerprint, not a gate
            fingerprint[f"{package}_version"] = "unavailable"
    try:
        fingerprint["git_commit"] = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "rev-parse", "HEAD"],
            cwd=ROOT,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            check=True,
        ).stdout.strip()
    except Exception:  # best-effort
        fingerprint["git_commit"] = "unknown"
    return fingerprint


def _ratio(seconds: float | None, l1_seconds: float) -> str:
    """Format `seconds / l1_seconds`, or `"n/a"` when `seconds` is `None`."""
    return f"{seconds / l1_seconds:.2f}" if seconds is not None else "n/a"


def _seconds(seconds: float | None, *, skip: str = "n/a") -> str:
    """Format `seconds` to three decimals, or `skip` when `None`."""
    return f"{seconds:.3f}" if seconds is not None else skip


def _mib(rss_bytes: int | None, *, skip: str = "n/a") -> str:
    """Format a peak-RSS byte count in MiB, or `skip` when `None`."""
    return f"{rss_bytes / (1024 * 1024):.1f}" if rss_bytes is not None else skip


def _table_header(*, measure_rss: bool = False) -> str:
    """Build the summary table's own header line (see `_print_table`)."""
    header = (
        f"{'value':>10} {'L_1(s)':>8} {'L_b(s)':>8} {'L_b/L1':>7} "
        f"{'G-off(s)':>9} {'/L1':>6} {'G-jit(s)':>9} {'/L1':>6} "
        f"{'V(s)':>8} {'/L1':>6} {'fastest':>8}"
    )
    if measure_rss:
        header += f" {'L_b(MiB)':>9} {'G-o(MiB)':>9} {'G-j(MiB)':>9} {'V(MiB)':>8}"
    return header


def _table_row(point: BenchmarkPoint, *, measure_rss: bool = False) -> str:
    """Build one summary table row (see `_print_table`)."""
    candidates = {
        "L": point.lineal_batch_seconds,
        "G-off": point.generational_jit_off_seconds,
        "G-jit": point.generational_jit_numba_seconds,
        "V": point.vector_seconds,
    }
    real_candidates = {
        name: seconds for name, seconds in candidates.items() if seconds is not None
    }
    fastest = min(real_candidates, key=lambda name: real_candidates[name])
    l1 = point.lineal_single_seconds
    row = (
        f"{point.swept_value:>10.4g} "
        f"{l1:>8.3f} "
        f"{point.lineal_batch_seconds:>8.3f} "
        f"{point.lineal_batch_seconds / l1:>7.2f} "
        f"{_seconds(point.generational_jit_off_seconds):>9} "
        f"{_ratio(point.generational_jit_off_seconds, l1):>6} "
        f"{_seconds(point.generational_jit_numba_seconds):>9} "
        f"{_ratio(point.generational_jit_numba_seconds, l1):>6} "
        f"{_seconds(point.vector_seconds, skip='skip'):>8} "
        f"{_ratio(point.vector_seconds, l1):>6} "
        f"{fastest:>8}"
    )
    if measure_rss:
        row += (
            f" {_mib(point.lineal_batch_rss_bytes):>9}"
            f" {_mib(point.generational_jit_off_rss_bytes):>9}"
            f" {_mib(point.generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes):>9}"
            f" {_mib(point.vector_rss_bytes, skip='skip'):>8}"
        )
    return row


def _print_table(points: list[BenchmarkPoint], *, measure_rss: bool = False) -> None:
    """Print a human-readable summary table to stdout, all at once.

    `L_1` is a pure normalization divisor (every `/L_1` column is
    relative to it) — `L_batch` is the real fourth engine series,
    `"lineal"` run at the same batch size as every other engine here,
    so its own scaling is visible on the same axis as G and V rather
    than only implicit in what they are divided by. `measure_rss` adds
    four more columns (one peak-RSS figure per backend, in MiB) only
    when `--measure-rss` was actually given -- gated on that flag, not
    on inspecting whether any point happens to carry real RSS numbers,
    so a run with every point skipped (e.g. every point outside Backend
    V's own scope) still shows the columns it asked for, correctly all
    `n/a`, rather than silently reverting to the narrower table.

    `main` no longer calls this directly for its own live progress --
    it prints the header once and each row as soon as that point is
    measured (`_table_header`/`_table_row`, both `flush=True`), so a
    multi-hour or multi-day sweep's own redirected log shows real
    progress via `tail -f` rather than staying empty until the whole
    run exits (stdout is fully buffered, not line-buffered, once
    redirected to a file — a genuinely long sweep's own log otherwise
    looks indistinguishable from a hang for its entire duration). This
    function stays for any other caller that wants the complete table
    built from an already-finished `points` list in one call.
    """
    header = _table_header(measure_rss=measure_rss)
    print(header)
    print("-" * len(header))
    for point in points:
        print(_table_row(point, measure_rss=measure_rss))


def _render_data(
    field: SweepField,
    points: list[BenchmarkPoint],
    fingerprint: dict[str, object],
    fixed: dict[str, object],
) -> dict[str, object]:
    """Render the full machine-readable benchmark payload."""

    def _ratio_or_none(seconds: float | None, l1_seconds: float) -> float | None:
        return seconds / l1_seconds if seconds is not None else None

    return {
        # Bumped from 2: every point payload now carries four more
        # `*_rss_bytes` fields (`None` on every field when `--measure-
        # rss` was not given, matching this schema's own existing
        # `vector_seconds`/`vector_skip_reason` "None means not this
        # run" precedent) -- nothing existing changed shape or meaning,
        # so no reader of schema_version 2 payloads needs updating, but
        # the payload a schema_version 2 reader would not recognize
        # every key of is worth flagging, not silently reusing the same
        # version number for a genuinely larger shape.
        "schema_version": 3,
        "generator": {
            "name": "Claude Code",
            "version": "Claude Sonnet 5",
            "model_token": "claude-sonnet-5",
            "provider": "Anthropic",
            "generation_date": datetime.now(UTC).date().isoformat(),
            "responsibility": "other",
            "script": "dev/bin/benchmark-engines",
        },
        "fim_version": (ROOT / "version.txt").read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip(),
        "environment": fingerprint,
        "swept_field": field,
        "fixed_params": fixed,
        "points": [
            {
                "swept_value": point.swept_value,
                "d": point.d,
                "lineal_single_seconds": point.lineal_single_seconds,
                "lineal_batch_seconds": point.lineal_batch_seconds,
                "lineal_batch_ratio_to_l1": (
                    point.lineal_batch_seconds / point.lineal_single_seconds
                ),
                "generational_jit_off_seconds": point.generational_jit_off_seconds,
                "generational_jit_off_ratio_to_l1": _ratio_or_none(
                    point.generational_jit_off_seconds, point.lineal_single_seconds
                ),
                "generational_jit_numba_seconds": point.generational_jit_numba_seconds,
                "generational_jit_numba_ratio_to_l1": _ratio_or_none(
                    point.generational_jit_numba_seconds, point.lineal_single_seconds
                ),
                "vector_seconds": point.vector_seconds,
                "vector_ratio_to_l1": _ratio_or_none(
                    point.vector_seconds, point.lineal_single_seconds
                ),
                "vector_skip_reason": point.vector_skip_reason,
                "lineal_batch_rss_bytes": point.lineal_batch_rss_bytes,
                "generational_jit_off_rss_bytes": point.generational_jit_off_rss_bytes,
                "generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes": (
                    point.generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes
                ),
                "vector_rss_bytes": point.vector_rss_bytes,
            }
            for point in points
        ],
    }


def _point_from_payload(field: SweepField, entry: dict[str, Any]) -> BenchmarkPoint:
    """Reconstruct one `BenchmarkPoint` from a `_render_data` JSON entry.

    `_load_checkpoint`'s own helper -- `.get(...)` for the four RSS
    fields, not `[...]`, since a checkpoint written before `--measure-
    rss` existed (`schema_version` 2) has none of them at all, and a
    resumed run should treat that exactly like `--measure-rss` not
    having been given for those already-completed points, not error.
    `entry` is already-parsed JSON (`dict[str, Any]`, not `Mapping[str,
    object]`) precisely so this function can trust its own field types
    without re-validating a schema this same script also writes.
    """
    return BenchmarkPoint(
        swept_field=field,
        swept_value=float(entry["swept_value"]),
        d=int(entry["d"]),
        lineal_single_seconds=float(entry["lineal_single_seconds"]),
        lineal_batch_seconds=float(entry["lineal_batch_seconds"]),
        generational_jit_off_seconds=entry["generational_jit_off_seconds"],
        generational_jit_numba_seconds=entry["generational_jit_numba_seconds"],
        vector_seconds=entry["vector_seconds"],
        vector_skip_reason=entry["vector_skip_reason"],
        lineal_batch_rss_bytes=entry.get("lineal_batch_rss_bytes"),
        generational_jit_off_rss_bytes=entry.get("generational_jit_off_rss_bytes"),
        generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes=entry.get("generational_jit_numba_rss_bytes"),
        vector_rss_bytes=entry.get("vector_rss_bytes"),
    )


def _load_checkpoint(
    output_path: Path,
    field: SweepField,
    fixed: dict[str, object],
) -> dict[float, BenchmarkPoint]:
    """Read a prior (possibly partial) `--output` payload for `--resume`.

    Returns already-computed points keyed by their own `swept_value`,
    but only when the checkpoint's own `swept_field` and `fixed_params`
    exactly match this invocation's -- refuses (`ValueError`, a loud
    failure, not a silent one) to resume into a mismatched sweep rather
    than quietly mixing incompatible runs into one payload. A machine
    fingerprint intentionally is *not* part of that comparison: the
    whole point of `--resume` surviving a machine reboot or a
    `benchmark-queue` timeout-kill is that environment details (load
    average, `git_commit` if a commit happened to land mid-sweep) may
    legitimately differ between the run that started a sweep and the
    one that finishes it -- only whether the sweep *means the same
    thing* (same field, same fixed baseline/replicates/generations/
    trials/seed/measure_rss) matters for whether resuming is safe.
    Returns an empty dict, not an error, when `output_path` does not
    exist yet -- the ordinary "no prior progress" case every `--resume`
    invocation's own first run hits.
    """
    if not output_path.exists():
        return {}
    payload = json.loads(output_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    if payload.get("swept_field") != field:
        raise ValueError(
            f"{output_path}: checkpoint swept_field {payload.get('swept_field')!r} "
            f"does not match --sweep {field!r} -- refusing to resume into a "
            "mismatched sweep"
        )
    if payload.get("fixed_params") != fixed:
        raise ValueError(
            f"{output_path}: checkpoint fixed_params {payload.get('fixed_params')!r} "
            f"do not match this invocation's own settings {fixed!r} -- refusing "
            "to resume into a mismatched sweep"
        )
    return {
        float(entry["swept_value"]): _point_from_payload(field, entry)
        for entry in payload.get("points", [])
    }


def _write_checkpoint(
    output_path: Path,
    field: SweepField,
    points: list[BenchmarkPoint],
    fingerprint: dict[str, object],
    fixed: dict[str, object],
) -> None:
    """Atomically write the current (possibly partial) sweep to `--output`.

    Called after every point, not only once at the very end -- a
    `--resume`-enabled multi-day sweep killed mid-run (a `benchmark-
    queue` per-job timeout, a machine reboot, `Ctrl-C`) must never lose
    more progress than the one point that was in flight when it died.
    Writes to a temp file in the same directory first, then `os.
    replace` onto the real path -- an atomic rename on every POSIX
    filesystem, so a reader (or a process killed mid-write) never
    observes a half-written, corrupt JSON file; the previous, complete
    checkpoint stays valid on disk right up until the new one has been
    written in full.
    """
    payload = _render_data(field, points, fingerprint, fixed)
    output_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    temp_path = output_path.with_name(output_path.name + ".tmp")
    temp_path.write_text(
        json.dumps(payload, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n", encoding="utf-8"
    )
    temp_path.replace(output_path)


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    """Run the sweep named by argv, print a table, optionally write JSON.

    Dispatches to `_run_rss_worker` first, before building the real
    argument parser below, when invoked as `--_rss-worker ...` --
    `_measure_rss`'s own internal re-exec of this same script, not part
    of this tool's own public CLI surface (absent from `--help`).
    """
    if argv is None:
        argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if argv and argv[0] == "--_rss-worker":
        return _run_rss_worker(argv[1:])

    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    parser.add_argument(
        "--sweep",
        choices=["d", "N", "mu", "m", "loci-length", "replicates"],
        default="d",
        help="Which SimulationParams field to sweep (default: d). "
        "'replicates' sweeps batch size (n_replicates) directly.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--values",
        required=True,
        help="Comma-separated values to sweep across, e.g. 4,10,20,35,50,80.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--replicates",
        type=int,
        default=16,
        help="Batch size for the generational/generational-vector runs "
        "(default: 16). Ignored -- read from --values instead -- when "
        "--sweep replicates is given.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--measure-rss",
        action="store_true",
        help="Also capture each backend's own peak RSS per point, via one "
        "fresh subprocess per backend (roughly doubles this run's own "
        "wall-clock cost). Off by default.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--generations",
        type=int,
        default=100,
        help="max_generations for every run (default: 100).",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--trials",
        type=int,
        default=3,
        help="Independent runs per point; the reported time is their median "
        "(default: 3).",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--seed", type=int, default=20260902, help="Base RNG seed.")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--config",
        type=Path,
        default=None,
        help="Optional fim YAML config overriding the fixed (non-swept) "
        "baseline -- N, d, m, mu, locus length. Same format and validation "
        "as fim run's own config file; must describe exactly one locus and "
        "scalar N/m/mu. Whichever field --sweep varies is read from "
        "--values regardless, never from this file. Defaults to this "
        "tool's own built-in reference-scale constants when omitted.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--output",
        type=Path,
        default=None,
        help="Optional path to write the full machine-readable JSON payload. "
        "Written incrementally, after every point (not only once at the "
        "end) -- a killed or interrupted run leaves a valid, up-to-date "
        "partial file behind, at the cost of one small rewrite per point.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--resume",
        action="store_true",
        help="Skip any --values entry already present in an existing "
        "--output file, continuing a sweep a prior invocation was killed "
        "or interrupted partway through, instead of recomputing everything "
        "from scratch. Refuses (a clear error, not a silent merge) if the "
        "existing file's own swept field or fixed baseline/replicates/"
        "generations/trials/seed/measure_rss do not exactly match this "
        "invocation's. Requires --output; a no-op the first time a given "
        "--output path is used (nothing to resume from yet).",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if args.resume and args.output is None:
        parser.error("--resume requires --output")

    baseline = _load_fixed_baseline(args.config)
    values = [float(v) for v in args.values.split(",")]
    fixed = {
        "N": baseline.n,
        "mu": baseline.mu,
        "m": baseline.m,
        "locus_length": baseline.locus_length,
        "d": baseline.d,
        "config": str(args.config) if args.config is not None else None,
        "replicates": args.replicates,
        "generations": args.generations,
        "trials": args.trials,
        "seed": args.seed,
        "measure_rss": args.measure_rss,
    }

    done: dict[float, BenchmarkPoint] = (
        _load_checkpoint(args.output, args.sweep, fixed)
        if args.resume and args.output is not None
        else {}
    )
    remaining_values = [value for value in values if value not in done]

    print(
        f"Sweeping {args.sweep} over {values} "
        f"(replicates={args.replicates}, generations={args.generations}, "
        f"trials={args.trials}, baseline={baseline}"
        + (f", config={args.config}" if args.config is not None else "")
        + (", measure_rss=True" if args.measure_rss else "")
        + (f", resuming: {len(done)}/{len(values)} already done" if done else "")
        + ")",
        flush=True,
    )
    print(_table_header(measure_rss=args.measure_rss), flush=True)
    print("-" * len(_table_header(measure_rss=args.measure_rss)), flush=True)
    for value in values:
        if value in done:
            print(_table_row(done[value], measure_rss=args.measure_rss), flush=True)

    fingerprint = _environment_fingerprint() if args.output is not None else None
    warmed_up_generational_numba = [False]
    warmed_up_vector = [False]
    points_by_value = dict(done)
    for value in remaining_values:
        point = _measure_point(
            args.sweep,
            value,
            baseline=baseline,
            replicates=args.replicates,
            generations=args.generations,
            trials=args.trials,
            seed=args.seed,
            warmed_up_generational_numba=warmed_up_generational_numba,
            warmed_up_vector=warmed_up_vector,
            measure_rss=args.measure_rss,
        )
        points_by_value[value] = point
        print(_table_row(point, measure_rss=args.measure_rss), flush=True)
        if args.output is not None and fingerprint is not None:
            ordered_so_far = [
                points_by_value[v] for v in values if v in points_by_value
            ]
            _write_checkpoint(
                args.output, args.sweep, ordered_so_far, fingerprint, fixed
            )

    if args.output is not None:
        if remaining_values:
            print(f"\nWrote {args.output}")
        else:
            print(f"\n{args.output} already had every requested value -- nothing new")

    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
